    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "underline",
            "v": "Grief"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " flooded China’s social media in October 2025 as news spread that famous physicist Chen Ning Yang had passed away. Yang, a Nobel Prize winner and academician of the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Chinese Academy of Sciences",
            "zh": "中国科学院院士"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", died in Beijing on October 18, 2025, at the age of 103."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Yang was born in 1922 in Hefei, Anhui, during a period of poverty in China. He studied at Tsinghua University and later went to the United States to further his education. Yang became famous for his work on "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "parity laws",
            "zh": "宇称定律"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " with "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Tsung-Dao Lee",
            "zh": "李政道"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Their work challenged an old idea in physics and won them the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1957. He also worked with Robert Mills on the Yang-Mills theory, which helped establish the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Standard Model of Particle Physics",
            "zh": "粒子物理学"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". This is regarded as one of the "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "cornerstones",
            "ref": "v-cornerstone"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of modern physics."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Yang returned to China in 1999 and taught at Tsinghua University, helping students become outstanding scientists. His students described him as energetic, "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "humble",
            "ref": "v-humble"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", and calm, with a clear mind. Even in his 80s, Yang continued teaching. He encouraged students to do top-level research and use science to solve real problems in China."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Yang’s "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "contributions",
            "ref": "v-contribution"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " extended beyond research. At age 99, he donated more than 2,000 books, letters, and papers to Tsinghua. He said, “I hope Tsinghua keeps not only my scientific work, but also the truth about who I am.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "People online shared their sadness. One wrote, “He is a giant in science who shows that Chinese scientists can be great.” Another said, “Yang "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "served",
            "ref": "v-serve"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " as a bridge between Chinese and Western cultures through science.”"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
